module

public import Mathlib.Data.Matrix.ColumnRowPartitioned
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.ToLin

/-!
# The generator matrix of a parity check in standard form

Following Nielsen & Chuang, *Quantum Computation and Quantum Information*, §10.4.1, a linear code
can be presented either as the **kernel** of a parity-check matrix `H` (`H x = 0`, eq. (10.57)), or
as the **column span** of a generator matrix `G` (the range of the encoding map `x ↦ G *ᵥ x`, cf.
`Matrix.range_mulVecLin`); the two presentations describe the same code. A parity-check matrix can
always be brought (N&C Exercise 10.16) to the **standard form** `H = [A ∣ I_{n-k}]`.

This file proves **Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.19** (p. 447): *if an `[n, k]` code has a
parity-check matrix of the standard form `H = [A ∣ I_{n-k}]` for an `(n-k) × k` matrix `A`, then a
corresponding generator matrix is*
```
G = [ I_k ∣ -A ]ᵀ = ⎡  I_k ⎤
                    ⎣  -A  ⎦   (N&C eq. (10.59)).
```

## Main definitions

The code coordinates are split into the `k` message positions `κ` and the `n - k` check positions
`μ` (so the block length is `κ ⊕ μ`), and `A : Matrix μ κ R`.

* `Matrix.stdParityCheck A`: the **standard-form parity-check matrix** `H = [A ∣ I]`, a
  `Matrix μ (κ ⊕ μ) R` (`Matrix.fromCols A 1`).
* `Matrix.stdGenerator A`: the **corresponding generator matrix** `G = [I; -A]`, a
  `Matrix (κ ⊕ μ) κ R` (`Matrix.fromRows 1 (-A)`).

## Main results

* `Matrix.stdParityCheck_mulVec`, `Matrix.stdGenerator_mulVec`: the block form of the two encoding
  maps — `H *ᵥ z = A *ᵥ (z ∘ inl) + z ∘ inr` and `G *ᵥ x = Sum.elim x (-(A *ᵥ x))`.
* `Matrix.stdParityCheck_mul_stdGenerator`: **`H · G = 0`** — the algebraic heart of the exercise,
  `A · I + I · (-A) = A - A = 0` (the standard-form instance of N&C Exercise 10.18).
* `Matrix.stdGenerator_mulVecLin_injective`: the columns of `G` are linearly independent (`G` has
  full column rank `k`), because its top block is the identity — so `G` is a genuine `[n, k]`
  generator matrix.
* `Matrix.range_mulVecLin_stdGenerator`: **Exercise 10.19 itself** — the code generated by `G` (the
  range of `x ↦ G *ᵥ x`) equals the code with parity check `H` (the kernel of `z ↦ H *ᵥ z`):
  `range (stdGenerator A).mulVecLin = ker (stdParityCheck A).mulVecLin`.

## Implementation notes

The block length `n` is modelled as the sum type `κ ⊕ μ` of the `k = #κ` message positions and the
`n - k = #μ` check positions, so `H = [A ∣ I]` and `G = [I; -A]` are the honest block matrices
`Matrix.fromCols A 1` and `Matrix.fromRows 1 (-A)`; this avoids all truncated-subtraction
bookkeeping around `n - k`. The block-matrix API (`Matrix.fromCols_mulVec`,
`Matrix.fromRows_mulVec`, `Matrix.fromCols_mul_fromRows`) reduces every proof to the identity
`A - A = 0`.

The code is phrased with the raw `LinearMap.ker`/`LinearMap.range` of `Matrix.mulVecLin`, matching
the convention of the surrounding development (`Matrix.range_mulVecLin_addCol`,
`LinearCode.dual_ker_mulVecLin`): over a field these submodules are the parity-check code and the
generated code in the sense of `LinearCode`.

The coefficients range over an arbitrary commutative ring `R`. Nielsen & Chuang's binary codes are
the case `R = ZMod 2` (where `-A = A`, so `H = [A ∣ I]` and `G = [I; A]` are the all-`0`/`1`
matrices the book displays); N&C note in the exercise itself that `G = [I ; -A]` "also holds for
linear codes over more general fields than `ZMod 2`", and the elementary argument here — which never
divides — is in fact valid over every commutative ring.
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace Matrix

variable {R : Type*} [CommRing R] {κ μ : Type*}
  [Fintype κ] [Fintype μ] [DecidableEq κ] [DecidableEq μ]

/-- The **standard-form parity-check matrix** `H = [A ∣ I_{n-k}]` (Nielsen & Chuang, §10.4.1,
Exercise 10.19): the `(n-k) × n` matrix over `R` whose first `k` columns are `A : Matrix μ κ R` and
whose last `n - k` columns are the identity, with the `n = k + (n-k)` coordinates indexed by
`κ ⊕ μ`. The code it defines is its kernel `{z ∣ H z = 0}`. -/
def stdParityCheck (A : Matrix μ κ R) : Matrix μ (κ ⊕ μ) R := A.fromCols 1

/-- The **generator matrix corresponding** to the standard-form parity check `[A ∣ I]` (Nielsen &
Chuang, Exercise 10.19, eq. (10.59)): the `n × k` matrix `G = [I_k; -A]` whose top `k` rows are the
identity and whose bottom `n - k` rows are `-A`. Encoding a message `x` as `G *ᵥ x` copies `x` into
the message positions and fills the check positions with `-A *ᵥ x`. -/
def stdGenerator (A : Matrix μ κ R) : Matrix (κ ⊕ μ) κ R := (1 : Matrix κ κ R).fromRows (-A)

omit [DecidableEq κ] in
/-- Applying the standard-form parity check `H = [A ∣ I]` to a word `z` combines the message part
`z ∘ inl` through `A` with the check part `z ∘ inr` verbatim:
`H *ᵥ z = A *ᵥ (z ∘ Sum.inl) + z ∘ Sum.inr`. -/
theorem stdParityCheck_mulVec (A : Matrix μ κ R) (z : κ ⊕ μ → R) :
    stdParityCheck A *ᵥ z = A *ᵥ (z ∘ Sum.inl) + z ∘ Sum.inr := by
  rw [stdParityCheck, fromCols_mulVec, one_mulVec]

omit [Fintype μ] [DecidableEq μ] in
/-- Encoding a message `x` with the generator `G = [I; -A]` copies `x` into the message positions
and fills the check positions with `-(A *ᵥ x)`: `G *ᵥ x = Sum.elim x (-(A *ᵥ x))`. -/
theorem stdGenerator_mulVec (A : Matrix μ κ R) (x : κ → R) :
    stdGenerator A *ᵥ x = Sum.elim x (-(A *ᵥ x)) := by
  rw [stdGenerator, fromRows_mulVec, one_mulVec, neg_mulVec]

/-- **`H · G = 0`** (Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.19; the standard-form case of Exercise 10.18):
the standard-form parity check `H = [A ∣ I]` annihilates its corresponding generator `G = [I; -A]`,
because `[A ∣ I] · [I; -A] = A · I + I · (-A) = A - A = 0`. Every column of `G` is therefore a
codeword of the parity-check code. -/
theorem stdParityCheck_mul_stdGenerator (A : Matrix μ κ R) :
    stdParityCheck A * stdGenerator A = 0 := by
  rw [stdParityCheck, stdGenerator, fromCols_mul_fromRows, Matrix.mul_one, Matrix.one_mul,
    add_neg_cancel]

omit [Fintype μ] [DecidableEq μ] in
/-- **The columns of the generator `G = [I; -A]` are linearly independent** (Nielsen & Chuang,
p. 446): the encoding map `x ↦ G *ᵥ x` is injective, since the message coordinates of `G *ᵥ x` are
`x` itself (the top block of `G` is the identity). Hence `G` has full column rank `k` and is a
genuine `[n, k]` generator matrix. -/
theorem stdGenerator_mulVecLin_injective (A : Matrix μ κ R) :
    Function.Injective (stdGenerator A).mulVecLin := by
  intro x y h
  rw [mulVecLin_apply, mulVecLin_apply, stdGenerator_mulVec, stdGenerator_mulVec] at h
  funext a
  simpa using congrFun h (Sum.inl a)

/-- **Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.19** (p. 447, eq. (10.59)): for a parity-check matrix in the
standard form `H = [A ∣ I_{n-k}]`, the matrix `G = [I_k; -A]` is a corresponding generator matrix.
Formally, the code generated by `G` — the range of the encoding map `x ↦ G *ᵥ x` — equals the code
with parity check `H` — the kernel of `z ↦ H *ᵥ z`:
`range (stdGenerator A).mulVecLin = ker (stdParityCheck A).mulVecLin`.

The inclusion `range G ⊆ ker H` is `H · G = 0` (`stdParityCheck_mul_stdGenerator`). For the reverse,
a word `z ∈ ker H` satisfies `A *ᵥ (z ∘ inl) + z ∘ inr = 0`, so `z ∘ inr = -(A *ᵥ (z ∘ inl))`; hence
`z = Sum.elim (z ∘ inl) (-(A *ᵥ (z ∘ inl))) = G *ᵥ (z ∘ inl)` lies in `range G`. Together with the
linear independence of the columns of `G` (`stdGenerator_mulVecLin_injective`), this shows `G` is a
generator matrix of the correct rank `k` for the code. -/
theorem range_mulVecLin_stdGenerator (A : Matrix μ κ R) :
    LinearMap.range (stdGenerator A).mulVecLin = LinearMap.ker (stdParityCheck A).mulVecLin := by
  ext w
  rw [LinearMap.mem_range, LinearMap.mem_ker]
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨x, rfl⟩
    rw [mulVecLin_apply, mulVecLin_apply, mulVec_mulVec, stdParityCheck_mul_stdGenerator,
      zero_mulVec]
  · intro hw
    rw [mulVecLin_apply, stdParityCheck_mulVec] at hw
    refine ⟨w ∘ Sum.inl, ?_⟩
    rw [mulVecLin_apply, stdGenerator_mulVec]
    funext r
    cases r with
    | inl a => rfl
    | inr b => exact (congrFun (eq_neg_of_add_eq_zero_right hw) b).symm

end Matrix

end
